## Test the meta data features field. It names the features that the software
## which last wrote the super block maintains. DRBD needs it because a version
## that does not know a peer flag preserves that flag without updating its
## value, which would make the stale value look current.
##
## drbdmeta claims the features it maintains itself where it writes the peer
## entries -- create-md and the v08 -> v09 conversion -- and otherwise never
## claims more than the meta data already claimed.
##
## Test layout: 256 MB image, flex-internal v09 meta data, three bitmap slots.
##
## The test walks through, in this order:
##  - create-md claims the features drbdmeta maintains
##  - the field takes one of the three reserved words, leaving two
##  - dump-md and restore-md round-trip the field
##  - a feature drbdmeta does not know is cleared, because drbdmeta does not
##    maintain whatever flags that feature covers
##  - a dump taken before drbdmeta knew the field has no "features" line;
##    restoring it claims no features, so the flags it carries stay untrusted
##  - the v08 -> v09 conversion builds the peer entries itself, so their flags
##    are current and it claims the features
$ tmpdir=$(mktemp -d) && img=$(mktemp --suffix=.img)
$ truncate -s 268435456 "$img"
$ drbdmeta --force --var-lib-drbd "$tmpdir" - v09 "$img" flex-internal create-md 3 2>/dev/null | tail -1
New drbd meta data block successfully created.
$ drbdmeta --force - v09 "$img" flex-internal dump-md 2>/dev/null | grep '^features'
features 0x0000000000000001;
$ drbdmeta --force - v09 "$img" flex-internal dump-superblock --output json 2>/dev/null | jq -c '[.features, (.reserved_u64 | length)]'
["0x0000000000000001",2]
$ drbdmeta --force - v09 "$img" flex-internal dump-md 2>/dev/null > "$tmpdir/dump.txt"
$ drbdmeta --force --var-lib-drbd "$tmpdir" - v09 "$img" flex-internal restore-md "$tmpdir/dump.txt" 2>/dev/null | tail -1
Successfully restored meta data
$ drbdmeta --force - v09 "$img" flex-internal dump-md 2>/dev/null | grep '^features'
features 0x0000000000000001;
$ sed 's/^features .*/features 0x0000000000000003;/' "$tmpdir/dump.txt" > "$tmpdir/future.txt"
$ drbdmeta --force --var-lib-drbd "$tmpdir" - v09 "$img" flex-internal restore-md "$tmpdir/future.txt" 2>/dev/null | tail -1
Successfully restored meta data
$ drbdmeta --force - v09 "$img" flex-internal dump-md 2>/dev/null | grep '^features'
features 0x0000000000000001;
$ grep -v '^features' "$tmpdir/dump.txt" > "$tmpdir/old.txt"
$ drbdmeta --force --var-lib-drbd "$tmpdir" - v09 "$img" flex-internal restore-md "$tmpdir/old.txt" 2>/dev/null | tail -1
Successfully restored meta data
$ drbdmeta --force - v09 "$img" flex-internal dump-md 2>/dev/null | grep '^features'
features 0x0000000000000000;
$ drbdmeta --force --var-lib-drbd "$tmpdir" - v08 "$img" flex-internal create-md 2>/dev/null | tail -1
New drbd meta data block successfully created.
$ drbdmeta --force --var-lib-drbd "$tmpdir" - v09 "$img" flex-internal create-md 1 2>/dev/null | tail -1
New drbd meta data block successfully created.
$ drbdmeta --force - v09 "$img" flex-internal dump-md 2>/dev/null | grep '^features'
features 0x0000000000000001;
$ rm -rf "$tmpdir" "$img"
